---
/**
 * EXPERIMENT RECORD — Pretext 0.0.8. NOT PRODUCTION.
 *
 * This page used to be a live evaluation rig: it imported `@chenglou/pretext`,
 * composed text around an exclusion zone and benchmarked the library against a
 * forced-reflow DOM baseline. M03-B.1 removed the dependency from the project,
 * so the rig went with it and what remains is the record of what it measured.
 *
 * That is deliberate, and it is the only arrangement that satisfies both
 * halves of the instruction. The experiment is retained as evidence; the
 * dependency is not retained at all. A page that still ran the rig would have
 * to keep the package installed and would ship its 44.8 KB into `dist/` on
 * every deploy — which is the dependency remaining in production, wearing a
 * lab coat. Evidence does not need a runtime; it needs to be written down.
 *
 * The executable rig is preserved in git and can be restored in one command:
 *
 *   git show 8a5f95f:src/pages/lab/pretext.astro
 *   git show 8a5f95f:src/scripts/lab-pretext.ts
 *   git show 8a5f95f:src/scripts/editorial-field.ts
 *   git show 8a5f95f:src/components/lab/EditorialField.astro
 *
 * Every number quoted below was produced by that rig on this machine and is
 * recorded in PROJECT_PROGRESS §1A.5 and §1B.1. Nothing here is estimated.
 */
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '@styles/lab.css';
---

<BaseLayout title="Pretext — experiment record" world="home" bare>
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" slot="head" />

  <main class="lab" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <p class="lab__stamp">Experiment record · not production</p>
    <h1 class="lab__title">Pretext 0.0.8 — evaluated, then removed</h1>
    <p class="lab__intro">
      Pretext was evaluated against the actual Home composition. The live
      composition did not require variable-width line widths, so the approved
      load-bearing justification was not present. Native CSS provided the
      required layout. Pretext was therefore removed from production rather
      than retained as an unnecessary dependency. The experiment is retained
      temporarily as evidence.
    </p>

    <section class="lab__section" aria-labelledby="h-claim">
      <h2 class="lab__h" id="h-claim">1 · What the dependency was approved for</h2>
      <p class="lab__note">
        One capability, not speed: <strong>per-line variable width</strong>.
        The about statement was to be set <em>around</em> the wordmark, each
        line's width supplied by the caller from the wordmark's measured
        geometry. CSS has no equivalent — <code>shape-outside</code> needs a
        float whose shape is known in advance and cannot take per-line widths
        from a sibling island. The M02 record was explicit that this was the
        whole justification, and that if the composition were cut, the
        dependency should be cut with it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lab__section" aria-labelledby="h-measure">
      <h2 class="lab__h" id="h-measure">2 · What the finished composition actually did</h2>
      <p class="lab__note">
        Measured on the live Home page with the approved copy, at the
        composition's design width:
      </p>

      <dl class="lab__readout">
        <div class="lab__metric"><dt>Characters</dt><dd>84</dd></div>
        <div class="lab__metric"><dt>Lines</dt><dd>3</dd></div>
        <div class="lab__metric"><dt>Text band</dt><dd>104.4 px</dd></div>
        <div class="lab__metric"><dt>Masthead block</dt><dd>241 px</dd></div>
        <div class="lab__metric"><dt>Lines below the block</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <p class="lab__note">
        Zero lines fall below the masthead block, so every line is laid at the
        same width. A variable-width composition with exactly one width is not
        a variable-width composition. The capability the dependency was
        approved for was never exercised.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lab__section" aria-labelledby="h-replacement">
      <h2 class="lab__h" id="h-replacement">3 · What replaced it</h2>
      <div class="lab__panel">
        <h3>Home, now</h3>
        <pre class="lab__code"><code>.hero &lbrace; display: flow-root; &rbrace;
.hero__block &lbrace; float: inline-start; inline-size: min(52%, 40rem); &rbrace;
.standfirst &lbrace; text-wrap: balance; &rbrace;</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="lab__note">
        Better on the merits, not merely cheaper. The float works with
        JavaScript disabled, where the island could not. It resolves during
        layout rather than after it, so it cannot contribute to CLS.
        <code>flow-root</code> makes the hero reserve the masthead's real
        height, which fixed an overlap the absolutely-positioned version had.
        And <code>text-wrap: balance</code> removes the orphan that was the
        other thing the library was earning its keep on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lab__section" aria-labelledby="h-bench">
      <h2 class="lab__h" id="h-bench">4 · The benchmark, for the record</h2>
      <p class="lab__note">
        Chrome, this machine, 200 width-queries per pass, 3 repetitions,
        medians. The DOM baseline writes a width and reads
        <code>offsetHeight</code>, which is what forces synchronous layout. The
        library is genuinely fast; speed was never the question.
      </p>

      <dl class="lab__readout">
        <div class="lab__metric">
          <dt>prepare(), one-time</dt><dd>2.8 – 3.5 ms</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lab__metric">
          <dt>Width query, Pretext</dt><dd>4 – 10 µs</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lab__metric">
          <dt>Width query, DOM reflow</dt><dd>103 – 192 µs</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lab__metric">
          <dt>Ratio</dt><dd>16 – 26× faster</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="lab__metric">
          <dt>Break-even</dt><dd>19 – 34 queries</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="lab__note">
        Two figures that were wrong before they were right, kept because they
        changed the conclusion. The first rig reported <strong>1.8×</strong>,
        which would have failed the kill criterion — it probed an
        absolutely-positioned, <code>visibility: hidden</code> element, which
        invalidates almost nothing and reflows nearly for free. Moving the
        probe into normal flow moved the result to ~20×. And a single shot
        varied between 3.4×, 104× and 20× across three consecutive runs,
        because JIT warm-up and <code>performance.now()</code> coarsening both
        bite hard at microsecond scale; the rig took medians thereafter.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lab__section" aria-labelledby="h-restore">
      <h2 class="lab__h" id="h-restore">5 · Restoring the rig</h2>
      <p class="lab__note">
        The executable version is in git, not deleted. If a future world turns
        out to have text long enough for per-line variable width to matter, the
        experiment comes back with:
      </p>
      <pre class="lab__code"><code>git show 8a5f95f:src/pages/lab/pretext.astro
git show 8a5f95f:src/scripts/lab-pretext.ts
git show 8a5f95f:src/scripts/editorial-field.ts
git show 8a5f95f:src/components/lab/EditorialField.astro
npm install --save-exact @chenglou/pretext@0.0.8</code></pre>
      <p class="lab__note">
        Until then the project carries no runtime it does not use. See
        PROJECT_PROGRESS §1C.1 for the decision record.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
